{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0008-PASSAGE", "text": "Important Chemicals in Human Body\n• Human body is made of chemicals\n• Four types of important chemicals in human body\n• Water\n• Carbohydrates\n• Lipids\n• Proteins", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0008"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0008", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 8, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 8, "pdf_page": 8, "formatted": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 8 (PDF p. 8)"}], "slide_number": 8, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0008", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART03", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "Important Chemicals in Human Body", "Important Chemicals in Human Body"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0009-PASSAGE", "text": "Important Chemicals in Human Body\n• Water\n• Most abundant inorganic compound (60%–80% of volume of living cells)\n• Important functions:\n• (1) Useful solvent\n• Dissolves many substances for chemical reactions in body\n• Body’s major transport medium\n• (2) Stabilize body temperature\n• Example: Sweating\n• (3) Cushioning to protect organs\n• Example: cerebrospinal fluid cushions nervous system organs\n• (4) Lubricant to enhance movement\n• Example: synovial fluid in joints", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0009"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0009", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 9, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 9, "pdf_page": 9, "formatted": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 9 (PDF p. 9)"}], "slide_number": 9, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0009", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART03", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "Important Chemicals in Human Body", "Important Chemicals in Human Body"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0010-PASSAGE", "text": "Important Chemicals in Human Body\n• Carbohydrates\n• Simple sugars and complex carbohydrates\n• Molecules are made up of 3 types of atoms - C, H, and O\n• Three classes 3\n• (1) Monosaccharides: simple sugars\n• (2) Disaccharides: combination of 2 monosaccharides\n• (3) Polysaccharides: complex carbohydrates", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0010"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0010", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 10, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 10, "pdf_page": 10, "formatted": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 10 (PDF p. 10)"}], "slide_number": 10, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0010", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART03", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "Important Chemicals in Human Body", "Important Chemicals in Human Body"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0011-PASSAGE", "text": "Important Chemicals in Human Body\n• Monosaccharides\n• Contain 3-7 carbon atoms in each molecules\n• General formula: (CH 2 O) n (n: number of carbon atoms)\n• Common monosaccharides\n• Glucose (blood sugar):\n• Directly used by cells for energy production", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0011"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0011", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 11, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 11, "pdf_page": 11, "formatted": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 11 (PDF p. 11)"}], "slide_number": 11, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0011", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART03", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "Important Chemicals in Human Body", "Important Chemicals in Human Body"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0012-PASSAGE", "text": "Important Chemicals in Human Body\n• Disaccharides (Double sugars)\n• Combination of two monosaccharides by dehydration\n• Too large to pass through cell membranes\n• Must be broken into monosaccharides before entering cells", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0012"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0012", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "source_file": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 12, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 12, "pdf_page": 12, "formatted": "01 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L1 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 12 (PDF p. 12)"}], "slide_number": 12, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0012", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART03", "HHS3190M-L01-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 1 - Human Body and Chemicals (July 2026)", "Important Chemicals in Human Body", "Important Chemicals in Human Body"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
